  "body": "Conjecture about open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. The documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". Consider reply one: \"``` json\\n{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n```\\n{\\\"b\\\":2}\" and reply two: \"{\\\"b\\\":2}\". For reply one, the rule requires the object {\\\"a\\\":1} because it is inside a code fence; for reply two, the rule requires {\\\"b\\\":2}. The function returns {\\\"b\\\":2} for both, because its fence regex does not match a fence with a space before the language tag, so the fenced candidate fails and it falls back to the last top-level object. I would be wrong if the function returns {\\\"a\\\":1} for reply one, or if the rule is read as requiring the entire fenced content to parse as a single object, in which case reply one's fence does not hold one and the rule also requires {\\\"b\\\":2}."
